## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of streaming distinct-count with memory constraints and practical trade-offs between accuracy and memory.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog sketch
- register count m controls error
- per-element O(1) update, O(m) memory, mergeable sketches

## Code Example
```javascript
class HyperLogLog {
  constructor(m = 16) {
    this.m = m; this.registers = new Uint8Array(m);
  }
  hash(x) {
    let h = 2166136261;
    for (let i = 0; i < x.length; i++) h = (h ^ x.charCodeAt(i)) * 16777619 >>> 0;
    return h;
  }
  add(x) {
    const h = this.hash(x);
    const idx = h & (this.m - 1);
    const w = h >>> Math.log2(this.m);
    const leading = Math.clz32(w) + 1;
    this.registers[idx] = Math.max(this.registers[idx], leading);
  }
  estimate() {
    let sum = 0;
    for (const r of this.registers) sum += Math.pow(2, -r);
    const alpha = 0.7213 / (1 + 1.079 / this.m);
    return alpha * this.m * this.m / sum;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does choosing m affect relative error and memory? 
- How would you merge two sketches in a distributed setup? 
- What pitfalls exist with non-uniform ID distributions?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests basic algorithmic analysis and trade-offs between brute force and divide-and-conquer techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Time complexity and space usage
- Inversions problem and two standard approaches
- Trade-offs with input size and data order

## Code Example
```javascript
function countInversionsNaive(a){
  let c=0;
  for(let i=0;i<a.length;i++){
    for(let j=i+1;j<a.length;j++){
      if(a[i]>a[j]) c++;
    }
  }
  return c;
}
function countInversionsMergeSort(a){
  function sortCount(arr){
    if(arr.length<=1) return {array: arr, inv:0};
    const mid = Math.floor(arr.length/2);
    const left = sortCount(arr.slice(0,mid));
    const right = sortCount(arr.slice(mid));
    let i=0,j=0, merged=[], inv= left.inv + right.inv;
    while(i<left.array.length && j<right.array.length){
      if(left.array[i] <= right.array[j]) merged.push(left.array[i++]);
      else { merged.push(right.array[j++]); inv += left.array.length - i; }
    }
    return {array: merged.concat(left.array.slice(i)).concat(right.array.slice(j)), inv: inv};
  }
  return sortCount(a).inv;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend the merge-count to return the actual inversion pairs?
- What changes if the input is already sorted or nearly sorted?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of streaming cardinality estimation under memory constraints—a fundamental skill for building analytics pipelines at scale.

## Key Concepts
- **HyperLogLog**: Uses probabilistic counting to estimate cardinalities with sub-linear memory
- **Register updates**: Hash to determine bucket, count leading zeros, store maximum run length
- **Merge behavior**: Element-wise maximum of registers enables lossless parallel merging
- **Error bounds**: Relative standard error ≈ 1.04/√m; memory scales as O(m)

## Code Example
```javascript
class HyperLogLog {
  constructor(m = 128) {
    this.m = m;
    this.registers = new Uint8Array(m);
  }

  _hash(id) {
    let h = xmur3(id.toString());
    return splitmix64(h());
  }

  add(id) {
    const hash = this._hash(id);
    const idx = hash & (this.m - 1);
    const remaining = hash >>> Math.log2(this.m);
    const leadingZeros = Math.clz32(remaining) - (32 - Math.log2(this.m) - 1);
    this.registers[idx] = Math.max(this.registers[idx], leadingZeros + 1);
  }

  estimate() {
    const alpha = 0.7213 / (1 + 1.079 / this.m);
    const sum = this.registers.reduce((acc, val) => acc + Math.pow(2, -val), 0);
    return alpha * this.m * this.m / sum;
  }

  merge(other) {
    for (let i = 0; i < this.m; i++) {
      this.registers[i] = Math.max(this.registers[i], other.registers[i]);
    }
  }
}
```

